SYMMETRICAL CHORD CATALOGUE
===========================
The pivots for the near-symmetry engine. A chord that sits one or
two semitones from anything on this list inherits that symmetry as
an efficient voice leading.

    A -> S -> F(A)      glue, then delete S
    result: A -> F(A)

What F is depends on which kind of symmetry S has.

    T-symmetric S  ->  smooth link between A and its TRANSPOSITION
    I-symmetric S  ->  smooth link between A and its INVERSION
    P-symmetric S  ->  smooth link between A and ITSELF, reordered


===========================================================
PART 1 — TRANSPOSITIONALLY SYMMETRICAL CHORDS
===========================================================
A chord unchanged by some Tx. Either it divides the octave evenly,
or it decomposes into equal-size subsets that do. In 12-TET the
only options come from divisors of 12: 6, 4, 3, 2.

These are exactly the "limited transposition" collections — fewer
distinct transpositions means more shared tones between them, and
that shared material IS the smooth voice leading.

  SET             NAME                 INVAR.  DISTINCT  BUYS YOU
                                       UNDER   TRANSP.
  --------------  -------------------  ------  --------  ---------
  {0,6}           tritone              T6         6      T6 links
  {0,4,8}         augmented triad      T4         4      T4/T8 links
  {0,3,6,9}       diminished seventh   T3         3      T3/T6/T9
  {0,2,6,8}       French sixth (7b5)   T6         6      T6 links
  {0,1,6,7}       double tritone       T6         6      T6 links
  {0,2,4,6,8,10}  whole-tone scale     T2         2      T2 links
  {0,1,4,5,8,9}   hexatonic/aug scale  T4         4      T4/T8 links
  {0,1,2,6,7,8}   double cluster       T6         6      T6 links
  {0,1,3,4,6,7,9,10}  octatonic        T3         3      T3/T6/T9
  chromatic aggregate                  T1         1      everything

THE THREE T6 TETRACHORDS. Any pair of tritones is T6-symmetric.
Up to transposition there are exactly three:

    {0,1,6,7}   tritones a semitone apart
    {0,2,6,8}   tritones a whole step apart   (= French sixth)
    {0,3,6,9}   tritones a minor third apart  (= dim seventh)

You can build a T6-symmetric chord of any size the same way: take
any set of pitch classes, add the tritone of each.

RULE: the distance you can travel smoothly = the invariance
interval. Near an augmented triad, major thirds are cheap. Near a
diminished seventh, minor thirds and tritones are cheap. Near
whole-tone, whole steps are cheap.


===========================================================
PART 2 — INVERSIONALLY SYMMETRICAL CHORDS
===========================================================
Notes arranged around an axis crossing the pitch-class circle at
two antipodal points. Notes ON the axis are free (they're fixed by
the inversion); every note OFF the axis needs a partner at the
mirror position.

    HOW TO BUILD ONE
    1. choose two antipodal points as axis, e.g. {D, Ab}
    2. put notes on either axis point, or both, or neither
    3. for every other note, add its mirror on the far side
    The axis may fall between the keys (e.g. between Eb and E).

    HOW TO TEST ONE
    Find c such that (c - x) maps the set to itself.
    Try c = twice any member, and c = the sum of any two members.

COMMON I-SYMMETRIC SETS

  dyads
    every dyad is inversionally symmetric — no exceptions

  trichords
    {0,1,2}   chromatic cluster
    {0,2,4}   whole-tone trichord
    {0,3,6}   diminished triad
    {0,4,8}   augmented triad
    {0,5,7}   quartal/quintal trichord (sus)
    {0,2,7}   sus2 / stacked fifth-and-second

  tetrachords
    {0,3,6,9}   diminished seventh
    {0,2,6,8}   French sixth
    {0,1,6,7}   double tritone
    {0,1,5,6}   "Z-cell" type
    {0,3,5,10}  quartal tetrachord
    {0,1,2,3}   chromatic tetrachord

  larger collections
    whole-tone, octatonic, hexatonic  (T- AND I-symmetric)
    pentatonic scale                  (axis on its "dorian" note)
    diatonic scale                    (axis on D in C major)
    melodic minor / acoustic          (axis on its tonic)
    any symmetric cluster or mirror voicing

  NOT I-symmetric — worth knowing
    major and minor triads (they invert INTO each other)
    dominant seventh, half-diminished, minor seventh
    harmonic minor, harmonic major

The fact that major and minor are NOT self-symmetric is exactly why
they're useful: an asymmetric object has a distinct partner to
travel to.


===========================================================
PART 3 — PERMUTATIONALLY SYMMETRICAL CHORDS
===========================================================
"P-symmetric" just means the multiset contains duplicate pitch
classes. Boring objects; the neighborhoods are not.

    {C, C}          unison / octave doubling
    {C, C, C}       triple unison
    {C, C, G, G}    doubled fifth
    any voicing with doublings

WHAT THEY BUY: an efficient voice leading from a chord to ITSELF,
reshuffled. This is the mechanism behind cluster music that churns
internally without progressing anywhere.

    (B, C, Db) -> (C, C, C) -> (C, Db, B)
    delete middle:  (B, C, Db) -> (C, Db, B)

Nearby chords: chromatic clusters, tight seconds, microtonal
detunings, tone-cluster textures, anything with two notes within a
semitone or two of each other.


===========================================================
PART 4 — SYMMETRICAL CHORDS THAT AREN'T ON THE KEYBOARD
===========================================================
Critical and easy to miss. The pivot need not be playable. It still
governs the voice leading of real chords near it.

    5 equal parts   {0, 2.4, 4.8, 7.2, 9.6}
                    -> the PENTATONIC scale sits near this,
                       which is why pentatonic collections glide
                       between transpositions so smoothly

    7 equal parts   {0, 1.71, 3.43, 5.14, 6.86, 8.57, 10.29}
                    -> the DIATONIC scale sits near this;
                       the one-note modulation D major -> A major
                       is this symmetry cashing out

Any n-note chord in 12-TET can be linked to its transpositions by
efficient voice leading only if it's close to the perfectly even
n-note division — playable or not.


===========================================================
PART 5 — THE PRACTICAL TABLE
===========================================================
Working backwards: your chord, its nearest pivot, what you get.

  YOUR CHORD          NEAREST S           COST   YOU GET
  ------------------  ------------------  -----  -----------------
  major triad         augmented triad     1 st   smooth to T4/T8
                                                 (third relations)
  minor triad         augmented triad     1 st   smooth to T4/T8
  major triad         tritone (2-note)    -      smooth T6, but
                                                 needs an extra voice
  half-diminished     dim seventh         1 st   smooth to its own
                                                 inversion = TRISTAN
  dominant seventh    dim seventh         1 st   smooth to T3/T6/T9
  dominant seventh    French sixth        1 st   smooth T6 =
                                                 TRITONE SUBSTITUTION
  dominant 7 #11      whole-tone          1 st   smooth to T2
  minor seventh       dim seventh         2 st   weaker link
  {0,1,4,6} jazz set  double tritone      1 st   smooth T6
  chromatic cluster   unison / doubling   1 st   smooth to itself
  pentatonic          5-equal division    -      smooth to T2/T5 etc.
  diatonic scale      7-equal division    -      one-note modulation
  quartal voicings    quartal trichord    0-1    already symmetric

Read across: pick the link you want, then find a chord near the
pivot that produces it.


===========================================================
PART 6 — USING THEM
===========================================================

FIND THE PIVOT NEAR ANY CHORD YOU LIKE
    1. write your chord on the pitch-class circle
    2. ask: which even division, which stacked-tritone set, which
       mirror set is one or two semitones away?
    3. that chord tells you the chord's secret smooth exits

THE EXTRA-VOICE TAX
When a larger chord borrows a smaller chord's symmetry, you must
add a voice. A triad exploiting the tritone's T6 symmetry becomes
four-voice:

    (C, C, F, F#) -> (C, C, F#, F#) -> (B, C#, F#, F#)
    tonal case: (C, C, E, G) -> (A, C, F, F)

Budget the doubling in advance rather than discovering it.

THE AMBIGUITY TAX
T-symmetric chords are rootless and centrically weak by
construction — that's the same property that makes them good
pivots. Augmented triads and diminished sevenths point everywhere,
so they point nowhere. Whole-tone has no leading tone. Pass through
them; don't camp on them unless dissolved centricity is the goal.

    to MOVE, use symmetry
    to ARRIVE, use asymmetry

DELETE THE PIVOT
The point of the gluing trick is that S disappears. If you actually
sound the augmented triad or diminished seventh every time, the
progression turns into nineteenth-century cliché. Compute with the
pivot, then write the two chords on either side of it.

THE CONVERSE, AS A DIAGNOSTIC
If you want a smooth link between a chord and its transposition or
inversion and cannot find one, the chord isn't near any symmetry.
No amount of revoicing will fix it. Change the chord.
